<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Myitnge River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dokhtawady, Dokhtawaddy River, Namtu River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Myitnge, or Dokhtawady, is a river of central Myanmar that rises in the Shan Hills and flows west to join the Irrawaddy just south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>. At the confluence stood Ava, or Inwa, capital of Burmese kingdoms for long periods between 1365 and 1842, whose site was protected by the two rivers and a canal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/myitnge-river.jpg" alt="Myitnge River" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wagaung · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:River_Myitnge.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Myitnge is a major tributary of the Irrawaddy, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irrawaddy-river">Ayeyarwady</a>. Its Burmese name and the Pali name Dokhtawaddy both mean little river, in contrast with the Ayeyarwady, the big river. It rises on the watershed between the Irrawaddy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salween">Salween</a>, not far from the Salween itself, and flows west through the northern Shan Plateau, where it is called the Namtu River, past towns such as Namtu, Hsenwi and Hsipaw.</p><p>Near its mouth the river divides. The Myittha branch leaves it to the west, while the eastern stream keeps the name Myitnge and enters the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irrawaddy-river">Ayeyarwady</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarapura">Amarapura</a>; the old capital of the Ava Kingdom was built between the two. The Zawgyi River joins it about 20 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyaukse">Kyaukse</a> at Nyaungbintha, and the Panlaung River near Ava.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On its north bank stands the Shwe Sar Yan Pagoda, built in 1053 by the Shan princess Sao Mon Hla on her way home after marrying and then leaving King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anawrahta">Anawrahta</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagan">Bagan</a>; its festival begins each year just before the full moon of Tabaung, in March. Anawrahta built weirs and canals on the Panlaung and Zawgyi rivers but found the Myitnge too wild to tame. Three centuries later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thado-minbya">Thado Minbya</a> founded Ava at the confluence, within easy reach of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> granary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyaukse">Kyaukse</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the railway bridge over the Myitnge, on the main line from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a>, was a target of B-25s and B-24s of the USAAF from New Year's Day 1943 to late April, and again that December together with the rail yards at nearby Paleik. Two years later RAF Thunderbolts attacked it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>A new road bridge over the river, 700 feet long and 27 feet wide with a footpath on each side, was opened in 1999. A hydroelectric project, the Yeywa Dam, was begun with assistance from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> on the Myitnge about 50 km south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Myitnge River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Shan raiders sacked Pinya and Sagaing in 1364, Thado Minbya united the two courts and in 1365 founded a new capital at Ava, Inwa, at the confluence of the Irrawaddy and <b>Myitnge</b> rivers.</blockquote><a href="/state/ava-kingdom">Ava Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Hsipaw, Shan Thibaw, was founded as a Shan principality in the <b>Dokhtawady</b> valley of the northern Shan plateau; tradition dates its ruling house to the 14th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/hsipaw-state">Hsipaw State</a></li></ul></section>
